  "raw_label": "1",
  "texts": {
    "preamble": "Kalal Balraj vs The State Of Telangana on 9 December, 2024 Author: Juvvadi Sridevi Bench: Juvvadi Sridevi The Honourable Smt. Justice Juvvadi Sridevi Criminal Petition No.13325 of 2024 O R D E R:",
    "facts": "The offences alleged against petitioner- accused No.2 are under Sections 272, 273 and 336 read with 34 of the Indian Penal Code (for short 'IPC'). 02.",
    "arguments": "03. Learned counsel for the petitioner as well as the learned Additional Public Prosecutor would submit that the subject matter is squarely covered by a common order in Chidurala Shyamsubder v. State of Telangana 1 rendered by the High Court of 1 CRL.P.No.3731 of 2018 and batch dated 27.08.2018 Judicature at Hyderabad for the State of Telangana and the State of Andhra Pradesh, and placed copy of the said judgment for perusal. Learned counsel for the petitioner further submitted that this Court vide Orders dated 17.03.2021 passed in CRL.P.No.1945 of 2021 [LEAKAGE_MASK] the proceedings against accused Nos.5 and 6 in same CC i.e. C.C.No.1014 of 2017 and prayed this Court to quash the proceedings against the present petitioner who is arrayed as accused No.2. 04. In Chidurala Shyamsubder's case (supra), a learned Single Judge of the High Court, following the guidelines laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal 2, held that the Police are incompetent to take cognizance of the offences punishable under Sections 45 and 59(1) of the Food Safety and Standards (FSS) Act, 2006, investigating into the offences along with other offences under the provisions of the Indian Penal Code, 1860, and filing charge sheet is grave illegality, 2 1992 Supp (1) SCC 335 as the Food Officer alone is competent to investigate and to file charge sheet following the Rules laid down under Sections 41 and 42 of FSS Act, whereas, in the present case, the Police have registered the crime for the offences under Sections 272, 273 and 336 read with Section 34 of IPC.",
    "petitioner_arguments": "03. Learned counsel for the petitioner as well as the learned Additional Public Prosecutor would submit that the subject matter is squarely covered by a common order in Chidurala Shyamsubder v. State of Telangana 1 rendered by the High Court of 1 CRL.P.No.3731 of 2018 and batch dated 27.08.2018 Judicature at Hyderabad for the State of Telangana and the State of Andhra Pradesh, and placed copy of the said judgment for perusal. Learned counsel for the petitioner further submitted that this Court vide Orders dated 17.03.2021 passed in CRL.P.No.1945 of 2021 [LEAKAGE_MASK] the proceedings against accused Nos.5 and 6 in same CC i.e. C.C.No.1014 of 2017 and prayed this Court to quash the proceedings against the present petitioner who is arrayed as accused No.2. 04.",
    "respondent_arguments": "",
    "other_lawyer_arguments": "In Chidurala Shyamsubder's case (supra), a learned Single Judge of the High Court, following the guidelines laid down by the Hon'ble Supreme Court in State of Haryana v. Bhajan Lal 2, held that the Police are incompetent to take cognizance of the offences punishable under Sections 45 and 59(1) of the Food Safety and Standards (FSS) Act, 2006, investigating into the offences along with other offences under the provisions of the Indian Penal Code, 1860, and filing charge sheet is grave illegality, 2 1992 Supp (1) SCC 335 as the Food Officer alone is competent to investigate and to file charge sheet following the Rules laid down under Sections 41 and 42 of FSS Act, whereas, in the present case, the Police have registered the crime for the offences under Sections 272, 273 and 336 read with Section 34 of IPC."
  },
